Hey Ayden,

My first three ideas were tools that organize what NASA already knows. The
bigger move is to produce new knowledge that can later be proven right or
wrong. This is the one I'd bet on.
One-Sixth: the first fire forecast for the Moon base

*Pitch:* An AI research team reads NASA's full record of fire experiments
in space and rebuilds it as one dataset. It tests its physics against Earth
results first, then forecasts how materials will burn at the Moon's
one-sixth gravity in the base's oxygen-rich air. The forecast gets a
timestamp and a DOI (a permanent citation ID) before any fire experiment
has run on the Moon, so it can be scored when one does.
Why the gap is real (I checked each of these today)

   - *Moon base air is untested in lunar gravity.* The challenge brief says
   the base will likely use 30%+ oxygen at 10.2 psi or less. It also says
   NASA's physical-sciences archive (PSI) has no partial-gravity fire data
   yet. Two planned experiments, LUCI and FM2, are expected to supply it.
   - *The archive stops below the base's oxygen level.* BASS-II, one of the
   main solid-material studies, ran at Earth-normal oxygen or lower; they
   added nitrogen to reduce it. So the planned Moon base air sits above the
   oxygen levels in that record.
   - *NASA itself doubts its standard test.* BASS-II's stated hypothesis is
   that NASA's standard flammability test "may not be conservative for
   flammability in space."
   - *There's a prize waiting for this.* NASA announced the $1M Moon Base
   Student Challenge on Nov 8. One of its six categories is astronaut safet=
y,
   and registration closes Jan 25, 2027.

How it works

   1. *Gather.* PSI's open API returns each study's test table and its list
   of publications, with DOIs: 17 papers for BASS-II, 10 for BASS, 16 for t=
he
   burning-rate study, plus the three Saffire experiments. Where a journal
   copy is paywalled, use the NASA-authored version on NTRS (NASA's free
   report server).
   2. *Extract.* Every test becomes one row: material, oxygen, pressure,
   airflow, gravity, what happened, and a citation. Two models extract
   separately. Disagreements get re-checked, and any still unresolved are
   marked "disputed."
   3. *Prove it on Earth first.* The papers already contain models that
   link gravity levels; one is titled "Simplified model for predicting
   difference between flammability limits of a thin material in normal grav=
ity
   and microgravity." The AI turns these into code, feeds them the space da=
ta,
   and predicts Earth results it hasn't seen. Only models that pass this ch=
eck
   get to forecast the Moon. This step is what makes it science rather than=
 AI
   speculation, and it's the best 10 seconds of the demo.
   4. *Forecast.* Run the surviving models at 0.16 g in the Moon base
   atmosphere. Uncertainty bands widen where the data runs out, above
   Earth-normal oxygen.
   5. *Attack.* A separate reviewer model tries to break every prediction,
   looking for stretches beyond the data, unit errors and cherry-picked tes=
ts.
   Only what survives gets published.

Role Model Job
Librarian Haiku 5.5 Sorts NTRS search results and publication lists
Extractors Opus 5.5, one per study, in parallel Turn tables and papers into
test rows
Theorist Fable 5.2, high effort Codes the published models, runs the Earth
check, makes the forecast
Reviewer Opus 5.5, separate context Tries to break each prediction
Writer Fable 5.2 Forecast report and draft fire rules, every line cited
Us (two people) =E2=80=94 Approve modeling choices, spot-check 10% of rows,=
 own the
story

Rough API cost: $100=E2=80=93250.
What ships by Sunday 11:30 PM

   - *The dataset:* every test from BASS, BASS-II and Saffire I=E2=80=93III=
, public
   domain on GitHub.
   - *A gravity slider:* drag from orbit to the Moon to Earth and watch the
   line between "burns" and "won't burn" move, with the Earth check shown.
   - *The forecast:* one or two well-studied materials, starting with thin
   acrylic plastic (PMMA), registered on Zenodo for a DOI.
   - *The 30-second video:* "No fire experiment has flown to the Moon. NASA
   plans a base there with oxygen-rich air. Our model predicted Earth resul=
ts
   it had never seen. Then we asked it about the Moon."

*Fallback:* Decide go or no-go at Sunday noon. If no model passes the Earth
check, drop the forecast and submit the dataset and gap map, which is Fire
Envelope from before and already a strong entry. The ambitious version
shrinks into a solid project instead of nothing.

*After the weekend:* Add smoke detection (the SAME experiments) and fire
suppression (FLEX) to cover prevention, detection and suppression. Turn it
into a Moon Base Challenge entry under astronaut safety. Score the forecast
when lunar data arrives. That's six months of fellowship work with a
built-in finish line.
Risks

   - *Paywalled papers.* Pull facts like test conditions and results, use
   NTRS copies where they exist, and don't reproduce the papers' text.
   - *The oxygen gap.* Forecasting above Earth-normal oxygen is an
   extrapolation. Showing exactly where the evidence stops is itself the
   headline recommendation: it tells LUCI and FM2 what to test first.
   - *Time.* This is a two-person job in the time left. Post on Space Apps
   Connect tonight for an engineering or physics teammate from the Cambridg=
e
   event if we need one, and one of us takes the site, story and video.

Two other big ideas I rejected

   - *10,000 analyses (Trend Detective):* a team of AI scientists runs
   every trend test across decades of NASA Earth data, and only findings th=
at
   convince an AI reviewer get published. It lost because most survivors wo=
uld
   be things scientists already know.
   - *Moon base in silico:* evolve Moon base designs against thousands of
   simulated missions built from NASA data. It lost because it doesn't fit =
any
   single challenge, which costs points on relevance, and the weekend versi=
on
   would be thin.

Sources

   - Space.com: NASA launches $1 million Moon Base Student Challenge
   <https://www.google.com/url?q=3Dhttps://space.com/space-exploration/nasa=
-just-launched-a-usd1-million-moon-base-student-challenge-to-award-cash-pri=
zes-to-students-that-solve-lunar-base-problems&source=3Dgmail&ust=3D1794874=
033353000&sa=3DE>
   - NewSpace Economy: Moon Base Student Challenge offers up to $1 million
   <https://www.google.com/url?q=3Dhttps://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/11/09/na=
sa-moon-base-student-challenge-offers-up-to-1-million-in-awards/&source=3Dg=
mail&ust=3D1794874033353000&sa=3DE>
   - NASA PSI combustion repository
   <https://www.google.com/url?q=3Dhttps://psi.nasa.gov/physci/repo/search?=
q%3Dcombustion&source=3Dgmail&ust=3D1794874033353000&sa=3DE>
   - Flame in Freefall challenge
   <https://www.google.com/url?q=3Dhttps://www.spaceappschallenge.org/2026/=
challenges/flame-in-freefall/&source=3Dgmail&ust=3D1794874033353000&sa=3DE>

Let me know what you think.

Charles

